WEBVTT

00:00.115 --> 00:03.731
This is Lang-SVG: hierarchical image vectorization with language priors.

00:06.343 --> 00:07.879
A photo is a grid of pixels.

00:08.148 --> 00:15.165
An SVG describes the image as a list of shapes, called paths. Lang-SVG redraws this living room with 256 paths.

00:15.435 --> 00:18.621
It stays close to the photo, at 29.7 decibels PSNR.

00:19.813 --> 00:23.857
However, existing vectorization methods, such as LIVE, return a flat list of shapes.

00:24.126 --> 00:27.429
Here are all 256 paths from LIVE, laid out one by one.

00:27.970 --> 00:29.318
Which of them make up the armchair?

00:29.587 --> 00:35.392
This is hard to answer. The paths have no labels, and they are scattered across the list. The picture looks right, but the file has no structure.

00:36.584 --> 00:39.307
Our key idea is to organize the paths as a semantic tree.

00:39.576 --> 00:43.968
Each node in the tree has three parts: a text tag, the paths that draw it, and a mask of its region.

00:44.237 --> 00:47.227
For example, the armchair is an object node under the image root.

00:47.497 --> 00:50.050
Its children are its parts, such as the seat cushion and the arm.

00:57.185 --> 01:02.310
So the tree goes from objects, to parts, to finer regions. Selecting one node selects all of the paths below it.

01:03.501 --> 01:05.591
How do we build this tree? We go from coarse to fine.

01:05.861 --> 01:08.271
First, object masks give the main objects in the scene.

01:08.552 --> 01:12.953
Second, an open-vocabulary part segmentation model splits each object into named parts.

01:13.222 --> 01:17.811
Third, Semantic-SAM adds finer regions. Each region is placed under the smallest mask that contains it.

01:18.080 --> 01:23.106
Fourth, L1-SAM-Prompting compares the SVG with the photo, and adds new regions where the error is large.

01:23.375 --> 01:27.490
Finally, visual refinement adds a few more paths for the remaining details, within the path budget.

01:27.760 --> 01:30.518
Every path in the final SVG comes from one of these stages.

01:31.710 --> 01:34.192
Next, we simplify each shape according to its depth in the tree.

01:34.461 --> 01:37.692
Nodes near the root are mostly covered by their children, so fine detail there is wasted.

01:37.961 --> 01:40.791
So a large node near the root is drawn with only a few points.

01:41.060 --> 01:44.078
A small node deep in the tree keeps more points, to preserve its detail.

01:44.347 --> 01:47.364
In short, the deeper the node, the tighter the simplification tolerance.

01:48.556 --> 01:52.467
After the structure stage, some details are still missing. L1-SAM-Prompting finds them.

01:52.736 --> 01:57.307
We compute the pixel error between the SVG and the photo. Bright areas show where the SVG is wrong.

01:57.576 --> 02:00.943
We take the largest error regions. Each one gives a box and a center point.

02:01.212 --> 02:05.077
We use them as prompts for SAM, which returns a clean mask that follows the edges in the image.

02:05.346 --> 02:08.436
These masks become new nodes in the tree. Here, five new nodes are added.

02:12.113 --> 02:15.346
Does the result depend on the quality of the mask prior? We test this directly.

02:15.615 --> 02:20.283
On the left, we use the 46 part masks predicted by an open-vocabulary part segmentation model.

02:20.553 --> 02:27.026
On the right, we use ground-truth part masks instead. Everything else stays the same: the same pipeline, and the same budget of 256 paths.

02:27.295 --> 02:33.195
The two results are very close: 29.7 decibels with predicted masks, and 29.8 with ground-truth masks.

02:33.464 --> 02:38.776
For parts like the sofa's back pillow, the coffee table's top and the armchair's back, the paths follow the masks in both cases.

02:41.922 --> 02:47.466
On the ADE20K benchmark, Lang-SVG recovers 79.3 percent of the annotated parts.

02:48.658 --> 02:49.809
Here are four more cases.

02:50.078 --> 02:54.015
On the left are the open-vocabulary part masks, and on the right the Lang-SVG result.

02:54.284 --> 02:57.141
The highlighted parts show that the paths follow the segmentation masks.

03:01.435 --> 03:05.534
Segmentation cannot name every region. So in the last step, we name the unlabeled nodes.

03:05.803 --> 03:14.222
For each unlabeled node, we ask a multimodal language model, Qwen 3.5. We give it the full image, a crop of the node, the color of its paths, and the tags of its neighbors in the tree.

03:14.491 --> 03:17.874
To test this, we hide some real part labels, and ask the model to recover them.

03:18.143 --> 03:22.188
The model predicts each label and explains its choice. Here, it gets four of five right.

03:30.446 --> 03:36.150
On ADE20K-Part, the tree context raises the accuracy from 58.5 to 81.3 percent.

03:37.342 --> 03:38.949
With this tree, editing becomes simple.

03:39.218 --> 03:42.280
One query selects the armchair, together with all of its paths.

03:42.549 --> 03:45.816
We can also recolor single parts by name, such as the armchair's seat cushion.

03:46.085 --> 03:47.130
Or the sofa's back pillow.

03:48.392 --> 03:49.186
Or the lamp's column.

03:51.314 --> 03:52.421
We can move the whole armchair,

03:53.467 --> 03:54.699
or delete it, in one step.

03:59.618 --> 04:03.429
Now we compare with other methods, using the same budget of 256 paths.

04:03.698 --> 04:08.574
The images look similar. But only Lang-SVG knows which paths form the sofa, the armchair and the coffee table.

04:08.843 --> 04:12.413
We also evaluate on the ADE20K benchmark, at three budgets.

04:12.683 --> 04:18.164
Lang-SVG gives the best reconstruction at every budget, measured by SSIM, PSNR and LPIPS.

04:18.603 --> 04:28.334
Its structure is much better as well. It has the highest StructXLIP score, and a much higher part recall: 64.2 percent at 2304 points, against 49.1 for the best baseline.

04:29.526 --> 04:32.142
Here are more results, from the photo to Lang-SVG.

04:39.599 --> 04:40.313
To summarize.

04:40.582 --> 04:53.831
Lang-SVG represents an image as a semantic tree of objects, parts and paths. It builds the tree from segmentation priors, refines it with L1-SAM-Prompting, and names the remaining regions with a multimodal language model. And we evaluate not only pixels, but also structure.

04:54.100 --> 04:54.770
Thanks for watching.
